---
source: crates/lighthouse-report/tests/it/agent.rs
expression: "render_with(Format::Agent, &findings, &gap(), &briefing)"
---
design/coupling  warn  internal/jit/compile.go:350:1
  owner:       jit::compile#function
  message:     function coordinates 14 collaborators in its package
  requirement: Coupling SHOULD be judged from direct intra-package fan-in and fan-out, that is the functions and methods of the same package that call a symbol or are called by it, against deliberately high thresholds. Calls from other packages and from tests do not count: a widely used public function is a service, not a hub.
  context:     A dependency hub changes for many reasons. Rust: The unit is the Rust module (a `mod` and every inline module are distinct units). Methods count with their impl's type; calls through a receiver whose type is not written in the code are not seen, so fan counts are lower bounds.
  evidence:    callees=["a","b c"] fan_in=2 fan_out=14
  expected:    valid go example `ordinary-fan` (sample.go), canonical
                 package sample

                 func hub() {
                 	left()
                 	right()
                 }

                 func left()  {}
                 func right() {}

                 func a() { hub() }
                 func b() { hub() }
  fingerprint: 5da1c26cc609
  resolve:     lighthouse review resolve 5da1c26cc609 --judgment <pass|fail|notApplicable> [--suppress <justification>] [--reason <text>] --attributed-to agent:<id>

design/no-panic  info  src/lib.rs:7:1
  owner:       m::helper#function
  message:     private helper has one caller
  requirement: Panic MUST be limited to impossible programmer errors, `Must*`-style APIs, or documented hot-path invariants with one recovery boundary; normal runtime failures MUST return errors.
  context:     Panics bypass the caller's control flow. Every place production code can panic is reported, one finding each (forbidigo `^panic$`; clippy's restriction lints `panic`, `unwrap_used`, `expect_used`, `todo`, `unimplemented`, `unreachable`): Go reports a call of `panic`; Rust reports the `panic!`, `unreachable!`, `todo!` and `unimplemented!` macros and any `.unwrap()` or `.expect(..)` call (syntactically, on any receiver, as clippy does). The findings are candidates, not violations: impossible programmer errors, `Must*`-style APIs and documented hot-path invariants are allowed by the requirement and stay with review. Test code, entrypoints (`main`, `init`) and functions whose name starts with one of `allowPrefixes` are not reported. In Rust, a library that is not published and that only tests link in (no other package lists it in `[dependencies]`, and it has no binary) is test code. The decision is in the `strict` preset only, as clippy's restriction lints and forbidigo are opt-in for the same reason.
  expected:    valid rust example `rust-result` (src/lib.rs), canonical
                 pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<u32, std::num::ParseIntError> {
                     text.parse()
                 }
  fingerprint: f999fb1d4030
  resolve:     lighthouse review resolve f999fb1d4030 --judgment <pass|fail|notApplicable> [--suppress <justification>] [--reason <text>] --attributed-to agent:<id>

acme/custom  error  a.txt:1:1
  message:     custom finding
  fingerprint: c8dfae349df1

broken.go: incomplete: type error
judgments: fail=the finding is right; add `suppress` with a justification to leave it in place; pass=false positive: the code conforms; notApplicable=the decision does not apply here; a hint to narrow it
